<section id="service" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<div class="service-head">
<div class="text">
<h2 class="h2 tap">Take the panel off</h2>
<p class="dim">The voicing board and the switch board, every part where the service notes draw it, with the clap's trimmer TM, the circuit by serial number (<a href="#revisions">Board revisions</a>), and a scope on any pin of any part. <a href="/ro-drums-909/guide#service">SERVICE in the guide</a></p>
</div>
<div class="stamp">
<div><span class="count">62 / 75</span> <span class="spec">Pass</span></div>
<div class="checks">service-note checks</div>
<div class="dim tiny">e.g. the master out's 6 Vp-p. The other 13: <a href="#differences">Differences</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<figure class="shot">
<div class="mount">
<img src={service.src} alt="ro-drums-909 with SERVICE on: the TR-909's voicing board above its switch board, every part drawn where the service notes put it, and on the right the scope, the peak-to-peak and HAND CLAP LEVEL readouts, the trimmer TM with TEST, and the circuit by serial number" width="2430" height="1542" loading="lazy">
</div>
<figcaption class="caption"><div class="row"><span class="fig">FIG. 2</span><span>SERVICE: the voicing board above the switch board. On the right, the scope, the trimmer <span class="mono">TM</span> and the circuit by serial number, from the first units to the late board.</span></div></figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</section>

<section id="revisions" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<div class="text">
<h2 class="h2 tap">Board revisions</h2>
<p class="dim">Roland changed the TR-909 during production. The service notes list each change by the first serial number to have it (SN p.7). SERVICE’s circuit setting picks the unit by serial number, from the first units (360100) to the late board (426700 up, the default), and the model puts back the parts each later change replaced. Every other part is the calibrated unit’s, a late board. The setting is saved with the session. <a href="/ro-drums-909/guide#service">SERVICE in the guide</a></p>
</div>
<div class="table-box">
<table class="table revisions">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">From serial</th><th scope="col">Parts</th><th scope="col">Change</th><th scope="col">What it does</th><th scope="col">Modelled</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="num">371500</td><td>R495 (bass drum)</td><td>150 kΩ from Q6’s base to ground removed</td><td>Surface-mounted on earlier boards (SN p.11)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">381500</td><td>C9 (bass drum)</td><td>0.22 µF → 0.33 µF</td><td>“For expanding the TUNE range”</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">381500</td><td>C87, R306, R312 (tape)</td><td>C87 removed; R306 4.7 kΩ → 47 kΩ; R312 470 kΩ → 4.7 MΩ</td><td>Tape LOAD</td><td class="dim">No: the tape interface isn’t emulated</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">392600</td><td>R442, R440 (crash, ride)</td><td>33 kΩ → 10 kΩ</td><td>The sample clocks’ TUNE range</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">393000</td><td>D701 (power supply)</td><td>RD6.8JB2 → RD5.6JB2</td><td>The reset at a brown-out</td><td class="dim">No: the supply isn’t modelled</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">415300</td><td>R417 (rim shot)</td><td>12 kΩ → 3.3 kΩ</td><td>“For giving the voice more realistic sound”</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">415300</td><td>C134 (hi-hat)</td><td>10 µF → 0.01 µF</td><td>“For rolling off unnecessary lower frequencies”</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">415300</td><td>R392, R393, R495, C600 (TRIG OUT)</td><td>22 kΩ and 10 kΩ → 1 MΩ; a jumper → R495 1 kΩ; C600 0.1 µF added</td><td>Keeps the rim shot’s TRIG OUT pulse out of TAPE SYNC</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">415300</td><td>C500–C512 (jack board)</td><td>0.01 µF to ground at each jack added</td><td>“Noise is high in OUTPUT”</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">426700</td><td>C54, R194 (tom noise)</td><td>0.0022 µF → 0.0047 µF; 47 kΩ → 100 kΩ</td><td>“To emphasize attack of TOM TOMs”</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p class="dim small">The program ROM changed too: group A, with no version label (SN 360100–393899); Ver. 1, an EPROM or a mask ROM (SN 403900 on); Ver. 2, which fixes STOP/CONTINUE under MIDI clock. ro-drums-909 runs version 4.0 at every serial number. A version 1.0 ROM only makes the factory patterns, which 4.0 dropped (<a href="/ro-drums-909/guide#factory">the guide</a>).</p>
</div>
</section>

<section id="differences" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<h2 class="h2 tap red">What is still different</h2>
<dl class="diffs">
<div><dt>The bass drum</dt><dd>Its second harmonic is perhaps 3–6 dB strong against three units, and DECAY 0 a little short: down 20 dB at 93 ms, against 106.</dd></div>
<div><dt>The clap</dt><dd>Its fourth tooth is a spike where three units have a full tooth, and with it the tail at 60–250 ms differs. A fourth unit has that tooth 3–12 dB down: units differ.</dd></div>
<div><dt>The rim shot</dt><dd>Its output is taken from IC50a rather than the drawn IC50b: five units' waveforms say so.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Snare and toms</dt><dd>The snare's pitch bend is 3–10 % high in its first milliseconds. The toms' far tail is up to 0.14 octave short at −40 dB.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Units differ</dt><dd>The calibration is one unit's. Another unit's toms sit 14–92 cents higher, and the unit in the video takes its crash a semitone below the lowest CRASH TUNE here. Earlier boards come from the serial number in SERVICE (<a href="#revisions">Board revisions</a>), with the calibrated unit's other parts. Two fitted values lie outside their parts' tolerances: the bass drum's R44 (36k, standing for the click's real cause) and the cymbal clocks' 4011UB gates (1.72 times their typical drive).</dd></div>
<div><dt>The top octave</dt><dd>The snare and clap are 0.4–1.2 dB low at 10–16 kHz at their jacks, against the board run at 384 kHz. The hi-hat, crash and ride are 0.5–0.7 dB low at 15–20 kHz, against ngspice.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Service-note checks</dt><dd>13 of 75 fail. The snare's supply peaks at 4.1 V where the notes draw 5. The low tom's and closed hat's accents span about 8 and 6.5 dB where the notes' traces span 5.8 and 4.7. The hats' positive peaks are 1.2–1.5 times their negative ones against the traces' 1.9–2.0; the AudioRealism unit's agree with ro-drums-909's. The snare's snappy noise bursts as its trigger ends, which the traces don't show; the AudioRealism unit's burst is the same size. At the calibrated unit's TM the clap reads 4.1 Vp-p, against the adjustment's 2.</dd></div>
<div><dt>CPU</dt><dd>The voices run on two worker threads beside the host's. A busy kit takes about three quarters of one core of an Apple M1 Max at 48 kHz; every voice at once comes near a full audio block.</dd></div>
</dl>
<div class="not">
<h3 class="h3">Not emulated</h3>
<p class="dim">The tape interface (SAVE, LOAD, VERIFY, TAPE SYNC's input), the cartridge, TRIGGER OUT as a plugin output, the clock, start, stop and song select on MIDI out, the analog voices' own power-on transients (they start at their operating points), and the supply rails' ramp.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="made" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<h2 class="h2 tap">How it was made</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li>
<div class="step-n">01</div>
<div class="step">
<h3 class="h3">The firmware</h3>
<p class="dim">Roland's v4.0 program runs unmodified on an emulated uPD7811, with the CPU board's battery RAM, key and LED matrices, MEAS/TEMPO display, TEMPO and TOTAL ACCENT pots, MIDI and DIN sync, driving the voicing board through its trigger latches and accent DACs. Every procedure in the owner's manual behaves as the manual says, the firmware's own quirks aside: 54 checks. Against a real unit's recordings, shuffle is within 0.84 ms at all seven settings and the 40 flam recordings within 0.7 ms.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-n">02</div>
<div class="step">
<h3 class="h3">Schematic and SPICE</h3>
<p class="dim">Every circuit of the voicing board and the jack board was transcribed part by part from the service notes into a netlist, which a circuit solver runs sample by sample: no voice is a hand-made approximation. Each netlist is checked against ngspice on the same circuit: the bass drum, snare, toms and rim shot within 0.12 % RMS, the clap 0.65 %, the jack board 0.35 %, the hi-hat and crash 0.45 % and the ride 0.6 % at their 192 kHz; the VCOs' pitch within 0.01 %. The noise generator and the hi-hat's and cymbals' counters, ROMs and latches are their logic, edge by edge.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="step-n">03</div>
<div class="step">
<h3 class="h3">Calibration to a real unit</h3>
<p class="dim">The schematic doesn't fix the pots' laws, the trimmer or the parts' spread. These were fitted to AudioRealism's recordings of a late TR-909 at every knob on a fine grid, each fit checked on settings held out of it, and on other units' recordings.</p>
<div class="table-box">
<table class="table fitted">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Part</th><th scope="col">Schematic value</th><th scope="col" class="fit">Fitted</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Bass drum C8</td><td class="num dim">0.33 µF</td><td class="num fit">0.40 µF</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bass drum R44</td><td class="num dim">22 kΩ</td><td class="num fit">36 kΩ</td></tr>
<tr><td>Snare VR7</td><td class="num dim">500 kΩ</td><td class="num fit">600 kΩ</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clap trimmer TM, wiper</td><td class="num dim">25.5 %</td><td class="num fit">30 %</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crash VR25</td><td class="num dim">10 kΩ</td><td class="num fit">8.36 kΩ</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cymbal clocks' 4011UB gates, drive</td><td class="num dim">typical</td><td class="num fit">× 1.72</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p class="dim small">With the schematic's values, the bass drum's log-spectral distance from the unit is 11.7 dB and the toms' tuning is 46–80 ¢ off RMS; fitted, 4.2 dB and 14–20 ¢. The clap's TM in the service notes, 2 Vp-p at its MULTI OUT, leaves the clap 6 dB under the other voices: its spikes are narrower than the unit's.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>

<section id="roland" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec">
<div class="text">
<h2 class="h2 tap">Roland's TR-909 software</h2>
<p class="dim">Its renders measured against the same real unit, at the same knob settings.</p>
</div>
<div class="table-box">
<table class="table versus">
<caption>Against the real unit. ¢ = cents.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col"><span class="sr">Source</span></th><th scope="col">Toms' tuning</th><th scope="col">Crash and ride clocks</th><th scope="col">Bass drum sweep, TUNE 75 %</th><th scope="col">Open hat at DECAY 0, to −20 dB</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="dim"><th scope="row">Real unit</th><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">14.4 ms</td><td class="num">136–137 ms</td></tr>
<tr class="ours"><th scope="row" class="mono">ro-drums-909</th><td class="num">within ±28 ¢</td><td class="num">within ±21 ¢</td><td class="num">14.5 ms</td><td class="num">136–137 ms</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Roland's software</th><td class="num">low 79–120 ¢ flat, high 150–210 ¢ sharp</td><td class="num">29–118 ¢ under</td><td class="num">20.9 ms</td><td class="num">31 ms</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p class="dim">Roland's software also places the clap's fourth burst 3–5 ms late, and its rim shot's tail is half as long. ro-drums-909 was calibrated on this unit; Roland's software wasn't.</p>
<p class="dim small">Measured with Roland's TR-909 software 1.1.3: single-knob sweeps around its default kit, at 48 kHz, MIDI velocity 127.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section id="rom" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec brief">
<h2 class="h2 tap">ROM images and voice mode</h2>
<p class="dim">The sequencer program and the hi-hat's, crash's and ride's samples are in ROMs, Roland's copyrighted work, so they aren't included. With the images from MAME's tr909 set, ro-drums-909 runs the program, <span class="mono">tr909_v4.ic609</span>, on an emulated CPU, and plays the samples from their ROMs. Without the program ROM, it runs in voice mode: the same voices over MIDI, with no sequencer. Without a sample ROM, that voice is silent. The version 1.0 program ROM gives a new instance the factory patterns. <a href="/ro-drums-909/guide#roms">The ROMs and voice mode</a></p>
</div>
</section>

<section id="artwork" class="sheet">
<div class="col sec brief last">
<h2 class="h2 tap">Panel artwork</h2>
<p class="dim">Roland's logo and the model names are trademarks, so their places are blank: three on the top panel and one on the rear. Each place takes an SVG or PNG file of your own, named <span class="mono">ro-drums-909-maker</span> and so on, in the ROM folder: drawn yourself, or generated with an AI tool such as Claude or ChatGPT. <a href="/ro-drums-909/guide#artwork">Panel artwork</a></p>
</div>
</section>

</Base>

<script>
// Underline the strip's link for the section being read: the last whose top has passed under the
// strip, or the last of all at the foot of the page.
const links = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('.onpage a')];
const targets = links.map((a) => document.getElementById(a.hash.slice(1))!);
const strip = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.onpage ol')!;
let current = -2;
function mark() {
  let i = -1;
  targets.forEach((t, j) => { if (t.getBoundingClientRect().top <= 80) i = j; });
  if (innerHeight + scrollY >= document.documentElement.scrollHeight - 2) i = targets.length - 1;
  if (i === current) return;
  current = i;
  links.forEach((a, j) => { if (j === i) a.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location'); else a.removeAttribute('aria-current'); });
  // Bring the link into view along the strip only. scrollIntoView would also scroll the page, up
  // by the scroll padding, since the stuck strip sits inside it.
  const a = links[i];
  if (!a) return;
  const r = a.getBoundingClientRect(), s = strip.getBoundingClientRect();
  if (r.left < s.left) strip.scrollLeft += r.left - s.left;
